00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.950 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  with no end in sight to the political  turmoil over brexit  the people have taken to the streets in  recent weeks  in significant numbers pro and anti-eu  protests have remained largely peaceful  but passions are running high  police chiefs are warning all those who  have a platform to speak  to be measured in what they say in the  days ahead  their words could have consequences we  do think it's important  that people who have that access to the  public  do understand the impact of what they're  saying and what says and it's  particularly  because what they say may give  permission or  be perceived as giving permission to  other people and it may be some people  we know the boundaries  aren't quite the same as some of those  people in  public office there has been an  increasing number  of threats against members of parliament  police say they are now offering extra  security and advice  to mps who've asked for help on a daily  basis the protests  outside parliament here are normally  pretty small in nature and easily  manageable from a policing perspective  but the longer the arguments over brexit  continue  the greater the likelihood of more  significant demonstrations  and more angry voices  [Music]  across the country police forces have  stepped up public order training  there are now some 10 000 specially  trained officers nationally  available within 24 hours to be deployed  anywhere in the country  but such an intensive policing  commitment would struggle to be  maintained  beyond seven days without impacting on  other core policing responsibilities  at a time when forces are already  struggling to meet the demands  of day-to-day requirements a ban on  leave  and calls on the military for help as  happened after the terror attacks in  2017  might all be required they could carry  out  duties that may normally be carried out  by the police  let's say area security duties guarding  buildings things like that  roles that the army could do in the  background  in order to free up more police officers  to carry out public order duties  in the event of a problem police chiefs  say they have no intelligence of any  planning for public disorder in the days  ahead  but with recent protests on the rail and  road networks  along with an increasing number of  threats to mps  they remain on high alert and warned  they'll deal robustly  with those who break the law mark white  sky news

